<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Swapped from a piston_engine model to an electric_engine.
     TRIED AND ABANDONED: the piston MD-550 model (magneto/starter/
     mixture-based FGPiston) never reached a self-sustaining RPM -
     confirmed directly (via ground-truth engine-rpm/thrust-lbs CSV
     logging) that it plateaus at ~1500-1560 RPM under starter
     assistance and decays smoothly to 0 the instant starter_cmd goes
     to 0, in BOTH the ArduPilot-integrated run AND the aircraft's own
     "validated" standalone reference script
     (scripts/geran2_catapult.xml) - this was never actually working,
     despite that script's header comment claiming it self-sustains at
     ~4,500 RPM. The displacement was already under-calibrated (see
     git history / CATAPULT_LAUNCH.md Known limitations #2) and even
     correcting that (300->450 in3, the value the old header comment
     said it should be) didn't fix the starter hand-off.
     Piston combustion timing/mixture calibration needs real flight
     data to fix properly (per CATAPULT_LAUNCH.md) - not something to
     keep guessing at. FGElectric (src/models/propulsion/FGElectric.cpp)
     has none of that failure mode: power is simply
     PowerWatts * throttle, with no RPM/spark/mixture/starter state to
     get stuck in, so it can't stall or fail to catch. Power sized to
     match this aircraft's own propeller (Engines/my_propeller.xml)
     design point: that file's own worked example computes ~24.8 kW
     shaft power at its designed full-throttle equilibrium (Cp=0.043,
     n=77.8 rev/s, D=1.0m); rounded up with margin for climb-out. -->
<electric_engine name="MD550">
  <!-- 38 kW ~= the real MD-550's ~50 hp rating, and matches this
       aircraft's own propeller design point: my_propeller.xml sizes the
       prop so that at full-rated shaft power (piston at 7000 rpm ->
       prop 4667 rpm via the 1.5 reduction) it makes ~650 N of thrust.
       Reproducing that same rated power with the electric model gives
       the same ~650 N available, which - against ~350-430 N of drag at
       the 40-45 m/s flying speeds this high-wing-loading airframe needs
       - leaves a healthy excess for the climb-out. The earlier 28 kW
       was under-rated for this prop and left the climb marginal. -->
  <power unit="WATTS"> 38000 </power>
</electric_engine>
